BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602a@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T102000
SUMMARY:Constraints of process migrations
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Containers and checkpoint/restore MC · Michal Koutný · https
 ://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2602/\n\nTriggered by:\nSubject: [PAT
 CH 0/4] bpf: add a few hooks for sandboxing\nMessage-Id: <20260220-work-bp
 f-namespace-v1-0-866207db7b83@kernel.org>\n\nProblem statements:\n- Users 
 (admins) are sometimes confused by some entity (PAM\, systemd\, container\
 n  runtimes) migrating their processes away from intended cgroup.\n- Coars
 e-grained DAC doesn't express well who (migrating process) can operate\n  
 on what (cgroup) to what (migrated task).\n- Limited immutability of membe
 rship assignment after certain point.\n\nProposed solution:\nBPF LSM hook 
 for cgroup_attach_permissions\n(combination with other existing migration 
 vetting mechanisms\nassociation of permissions with PIDs instead of UIDs?)
 \n\nAlternate solutions:\n- stick with regular cgroup FS permissions\n- ut
 ilization of other existing LSM hooks
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2602/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602b@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T102000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T104000
SUMMARY:Memory Tracking in forensic checkpointing: what soft-dirty bits can
 ’t tell us
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Containers and checkpoint/restore MC · Shailja Shaktawat · ht
 tps://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2607/\n\nCRIU’s incremental chec
 kpointing is being used for forensic container snapshot(Stoyanov et al.\, 
 DFRWS 2026) chains. Soft-dirty tracking cuts snapshot size by about 10× a
 nd makes high-frequency capture practical.  Live migration only needs a co
 rrect final state. Forensics needs the path that led there. Soft-dirty was
  built for migration and is now being reused for forensics.\n\nA forensic 
 snapshot chain starts with one full snapshot\, then stores only later chan
 ges. Hash-chaining makes tampering detectable\, as long as the host kernel
  and CRIU stay trusted.\n\nThis talk brings the discusion of the challenge
 s with the existing memory tracking mechanism and improvements for forensi
 c use.\n\nSoft-dirty answers one question: did this page change since the 
 last reset? It cannot say when\, how many times\, in what order\, or by wh
 om. Tracking makes pages read-only and catches the first write to each but
  a page written once and a page written ten thousand times are indistingui
 shable. Every intermediate value is gone\, along with any ordering or cont
 ext. Even when two pages change\, the kernel walks page tables.\n\nSome wa
 ys to improve this tracking for forensic checkpointing at container level\
 , have been inspired from several of live migration\, hypervisor specific 
 and hardware trapping techniques. One such direction might be maintaining 
 a kernel level dirty set of pages. PAGEMAP_SCAN already helps here\, but C
 RIU mainly uses it as a faster way to read soft-dirty. The tracking mechan
 ism is unchanged\, and the kernel-side still walks the range\, there is no
  maintained dirty set\, only a faster way to report the outcome of a walk.
 \n\nAnother direction can be to finally start utilising the first-write pa
 ge fault data rather than discarding almost everything it exposes. Today
 ’s interfaces force a choice: async uffd-wp is cheap but reports one bit
 \, while sync uffd-wp can report richer data at a userspace round trip per
  fault. Forensics needs rich-and-cheap: capture at the fault\, kernel-side
 \, without that round trip.\n\nA separate\, lighter option is to recover c
 hanged bytes by diffing a captured page against its parent and storing onl
 y the difference.\n\nAt last\, soft-dirty is not broken. It does what live
  migration needed\, well enough that forensic snapshot chains already use 
 it. The problem is that forensics asks a different question\, and the inte
 rface has no answer for it. Upstream work so far has made the answer cheap
 er to retrieve without making it richer.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2607/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602c@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T104000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T110000
SUMMARY:Enabling Incremental Checkpointing for GPU Workloads
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Containers and checkpoint/restore MC · Radostin Stoyanov · ht
 tps://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2603/\n\nWith the increased adopti
 on of AI workloads\, efficient GPU checkpointing mechanisms are becoming c
 rucial for inference\, training\, fine-tuning\, and reinforcement learning
  workloads. One of the key challenges with GPU checkpointing today is the 
 lack of memory-tracking support that enables incremental snapshots. When t
 he GPU state is checkpointed into host memory\, all pages appear modified\
 , preventing CRIU from identifying which pages have changed since the prev
 ious checkpoint and resulting in full snapshot of the GPU memory for every
  iteration. In this talk\, we will discuss extending the GPU plugins for C
 RIU with support for memory tracking that enables efficient incremental ch
 eckpointing. We will explore the benefits of this approach and the trade-o
 ffs between performance overhead and storage efficiency across different G
 PU workloads.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2603/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602d@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T110000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T113000
SUMMARY:dm-qcow2: device-mapper-based QCOW2 storage for containers
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Containers and checkpoint/restore MC · Andrei Zhadchenko · ht
 tps://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2605/\n\nContainer storage commonl
 y relies on directory overlays\, filesystem-native subvolumes\, or thin-pr
 ovisioned block devices. We will explore another approach: exposing QCOW2 
 images directly as Linux block devices through a device-mapper target. QCO
 W2 is the standard virtual-disk format across much of the QEMU/KVM ecosyst
 em. Its widespread adoption\, mature tooling\, and features such as backin
 g-file chains\, persistent bitmaps\, and sparse allocation make it an attr
 active option for container storage as well.\n\nBut just having a nice loo
 p device is not a complete solution. Container storage must also support e
 ssential operations such as snapshots\, backups\, and migration. This talk
  will show how we map these requirements onto existing device-mapper and L
 inux kernel capabilities\, what is still missing\, and which problems rema
 in unsolved.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2605/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602e@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T122000
SUMMARY:Upgrade restrictions for file descriptors
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Containers and checkpoint/restore MC · Jori Koolstra · https:
 //lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2604/\n\nFor quite a while there has b
 een a wish from container runtime to be able to restrict how we can reuse 
 a particular file descriptor. For instance\, CVE-2019-5736 showcased a pri
 vilege escalation in runc\, whereby the possibility of reopening `/proc/se
 lf/exe` as writeable allowed a malicious image to overwrite the runc binar
 y. That was patched on the user space side by copying runc to a sealed mem
 fd_create() file before executing the target. However\, it would be better
  if we could just set restrictions on how some file descriptor can be used
  (via procfs or O_EMPTYPATH) to reopen a file as writeable in the first pl
 ace. Thus we would like to have some kind of upgrade mask for file descrip
 tors.\n\nEffort has been spent in the past to implement something like thi
 s. For instance\, by David Drysdale in 2013 and later by Aleksa Sarai as p
 art of openat2(2) in 2019. I want to take some time to summarize some of t
 hese past ideas and see why they were unsuccessful\, and what makes this i
 n particular a hairy problem. We can finish with some discussion about wha
 t a merge-able patchset would look like.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2604/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602f@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T122000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T124000
SUMMARY:Checkpoint/Restore of Device Cgroup eBPF Programs
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Containers and checkpoint/restore MC · Pavel Tikhomirov · htt
 ps://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2606/\n\nAfter moving OpenVZ contai
 ners to cgroup-v2 we are struggling a bit to reach\nfeature parity with wh
 at we had before. One such feature is running nested\nDocker containers in
 side an OpenVZ (system) container — part of making our\ncontainers behav
 e as close to a regular server as possible.\n\nIn cgroup-v2 the device con
 troller was reformed drastically: device\navailability can only be control
 led by special BPF_PROG_TYPE_CGROUP_DEVICE\nprograms attached to cgroups. 
 Docker naturally relies on this\, and systemd also\nemploys it for its own
  services — so a migrated container without these\nprograms comes back w
 ith its device policy silently dropped. The problem is\nthat the BPF inter
 face is asymmetric: the kernel accepts a program\, but gives\nno way to ge
 t it back in a reloadable form. The verified/JITed instructions it\ncan re
 port are not portable — the verifier rewrites context accesses and helpe
 r\ncalls into offsets and addresses specific to the running kernel\, so th
 ey can\nneither pass verification again nor work on another kernel. Mainst
 ream solved\nthe same problem for seccomp a decade ago — commit f8e529ed
 941ba ("seccomp\,\nptrace: add support for dumping seccomp filters") added
  an API to retrieve\nloaded filters specifically for C/R — but for gener
 al BPF programs no such interface\nexists to this day.\n\nWe took the secc
 omp approach for cgroup device programs in the Virtuozzo\nkernel: keep a c
 opy of the original\, pre-verification instructions at load time\nand repo
 rt it through BPF_OBJ_GET_INFO_BY_FD in a new\nbpf_prog_info::orig_prog_in
 sns field. The encoding (struct bpf_insn) and the\nCGROUP_DEVICE context a
 re stable UAPI\, so on restore the program is simply\nreloaded and the des
 tination kernel re-verifies and re-JITs it. On top of this\nAPI\, CRIU now
  dumps programs and their per-cgroup attachments (via\nBPF_PROG_QUERY)\, t
 he bpf-prog anon-inode fds held by processes\, and on restore\nre-attaches
  everything once the cgroup tree is recreated\, preserving the\noriginal s
 haring topology.\n\nIn this talk we will go over the kernel and CRIU sides
  of the design and\ndiscuss whether such an interface could be accepted in
  mainstream\, along with\nthe open problems on the way to generic BPF chec
 kpoint/restore: original\ninstructions for arbitrary program types\, maps 
 and their contents\, links\, and\npinned objects.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2606/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602g@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T124000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T130000
SUMMARY:Addressing Challenges for Container Migration in Heterogeneous Clus
 ters
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Containers and checkpoint/restore MC · Andrei Vagin · https:/
 /lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2608/\n\nCheckpoint/Restore (C/R) is in
 creasingly used for both startup acceleration (restoring pre-warmed snapsh
 ot instances on demand) and live migration. However\, deploying static sna
 pshots or migrating tasks across heterogeneous clusters creates severe run
 time bottlenecks when source and target nodes possess differing CPU capabi
 lities. While CRIU and container runtimes can accurately preserve memory s
 tate\, handling CPU feature consistency and safe architectural state resto
 ration across diverse hardware remains an unsolved problem at the kernel b
 oundary.\n\nThis session will focus on two critical kernel/userspace inter
 action challenges:. While CRIU and container runtimes can accurately captu
 re and restore process states and kernel resources\, handling CPU feature 
 consistency and safe architectural state restoration across diverse hardwa
 re remains an unsolved problem at the kernel boundary.\n\nThis session wil
 l focus on two critical kernel/userspace interaction challenges:\n 1. HWCA
 P Inheritance & Feature Discovery: Examining feature detection failures wh
 en restoring snapshots on target nodes with different CPU features\, and r
 eviewing proposed mechanisms to inherit or mask hardware capabilities (HWC
 AP/HWCAP2 via auxv) across execve().\n 2. Restoring Extended Signal Frame 
 States: Analyzing edge cases where tasks contain in-flight signal frames w
 ith architecture-specific CPU state on their stack. We will discuss why ri
 gid kernel-side frame validation during rt_sigreturn causes restoration fa
 ilures across hardware generations\, and propose flexible validation strat
 egies that prevent state corruption while preserving ABI safety.\n\nGoal: 
 Align kernel\, container\, and language runtime maintainers on kernel-assi
 sted CPU feature control and flexible signal-context restoration to make s
 napshot-based fast-booting and live migration robust across heterogeneous 
 fleets.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2608/
END:VEVENT
